;;; A query's projection classes merged the int `1` and the name `"1"` — M1e2
;;; **W28**, register row `W28/classes-int-name`
;;; (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), banked by S1e2.3.8 (2026-11-06) from its
;;; step 1's probe, made smaller, on the user's ruling of that day
;;; (Q-M1e2.20 (a)).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One candidate relation, `pick`, over the objects `A`, `B` and the name
;;; `1`, which rule `b`'s string `"1"` makes a name of the program. Rule `a`
;;; concludes `(got 1)` over the int from `(pick A)`, rule `b` `(got "1")`
;;; over the name from `(pick B)`. The query asks `(got ?v)` over all of R.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Nothing refutes, so R is the eight subsets of the three candidates. A
;;; state's answers to the goal are `(got 1)` where it holds `(pick A)` and
;;; `(got "1")` where it holds `(pick B)`, so the states fall in four
;;; projection classes: none (∅ and `{(pick "1")}`), `(got 1)` alone,
;;; `(got "1")` alone, and both (two states each).
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; At `86227f9a` the classes were keyed by the goal's answers as printed
;;; text, in which the int `1` and the name `"1"` were one text, `(got 1)`:
;;; `ein solve FILE` said *8 states → 2 projection classes*, every state
;;; holding either rule's conclusion in one class, in a release build and a
;;; debug one.

(relation pick Node)
(is-a A Node) (is-a B Node)
(rule a () :match (pick A) :assert (got 1))
(rule b () :match (pick B) :assert (got "1"))
(query :goal (got ?v))
